(;GM[1]FF[4]CA[UTF-8]SZ[19]AP[Go Diagram Annotator\:4.0]PB[Go Seigen]PW[Hashimoto Utaro]BR[3p]WR[4p]RE[W+6]DT[1929-03-27]EV["Young Go Seigen's Progress" series sponsored by Jiji Shinpo]KM[0]HA[0]TM[黑\: 07\:16  白\: 04\:51];B[qd];W[ed];B[od];W[qp];B[eq];W[cp];B[oq];W[mq];B[mp];W[lp];B[mo];W[nq];B[po];W[or];B[pq];W[ql];B[qj];W[qo];B[qq];W[ol];B[pn];W[qn];B[jp];W[lo];B[mn];W[np];B[oo];W[ln];B[ml];W[pm];B[oj];W[nk];B[nl];W[ok];B[nj];W[mk];B[kl]C[By playing here (a Jump), make me feel like Black know he is in negative position.]UC[1];W[jo];B[ip];W[io];B[ho];W[hp]C[He played like me. ^^];B[hq];W[gp];B[gq];W[hn];B[do];W[fp];B[fq];W[jk];B[jl];W[ik];B[kn];W[jn];B[hm];W[fn];B[gm];W[fm]C[If me, I would played G6.];B[jm];W[kp];B[hk];W[co];B[dn];W[cn];B[dm];W[jr];B[cm]C[If me, J3.];W[dq];B[ep];W[fk];B[gk];W[er];B[cj]C[So he do Tenuki (手抜き)? [... to ignore an opponent's move in order to play elsewhere on the board\]]DO[];W[ej];B[ch];W[mc];B[jc];W[me];B[gc];W[pc];B[oc];W[ob];B[pd];W[qb];B[qc];W[pb]C[Did they do text book educational here GRP1={[R16, P16, Q17, P17, P18, Q16, R18, R17, Q18\]}?];B[nf];W[fc];B[gd];W[ce];B[nc]C[The Agent mentioned that this is a “Local response to Q18.” While that might be true, to me, this depicts Seigen’s stubborn attempt to win over Utaro at this quarter of the board.];W[nb];B[lc];W[mb];B[ld];W[md]C[This is undoubtedly the position that anyone would adopt.];B[rb];W[ra];B[sb];W[mf];B[nh]C[Seigen aims high here. He wants to take over this part={qrt2} of the board. See the connection he is trying to make with GRP1={[O12, O10, P10, R10\]} and GRP2={[O12, O14, P16, Q16, R16\]} ...];W[gb];B[hb];W[fb];B[kf];W[ng];B[og]C[Instead of N13, the position {here} would better secure the inner territory within this quarter={qrt2}.];W[mg];B[rk];W[of]C[Utaro didn’t follow Seigen’s tenuki move at S9. Show his strong desire to regain his influence here={qrt2}.];B[pg];W[oe];B[rl];W[rm];B[pk]C[Black move {here} is to destroying White's potential Eye-Shape={[R6, S7, Q7, R8, P8, {!Q9}\]}.

Before this move, White played Move 114 (S7), attempting to establish a stable local base or force a defensive reaction. By placing Move 115 {here} right in the middle of White's stones, Black is executing a vital placement (often called a "vital point" strike).

This actively splits White's internal space, ensuring White cannot cleanly partition this area into two separate, secure pockets of territory known as two eye (or Nigan '二眼').];W[lj];B[ir]C[Move 117 {here} is not tenuki. It is a direct local response following a sharp sequence of fighting.

• Defining Tenuki (手抜き)\: In Go, tenuki means ignoring your opponent's last move to play in an entirely different, unrelated area of the board.

• The Local Tactical Flow\: In the original position, Black attacked at 115 to destroy White's eye space. In response, White forced a counter-play by sliding/jumping out with Move 116 at M10.

• Black's Immediate Answer\: Instead of leaving the area, Black immediately plays Move 117 at J2 (at the bottom edge of the board). While J2 looks far away from M10, it is actively defending the base or cutting off White's connection to the lower side group, keeping the tension and the local life-and-death struggle alive.

